USOS 3.1.2-usos11 patch 0012 (2026-10-17): HPET and AMD GPIO hidden from Windows 2000 in the legacy DSDT/SSDT copies (Windows 2000 ACPI flow only)

Windows 2000 SP4 with the ACPI HAL on the ASRock X470 (no CSM) stopped in
kernel phase 1. Diagnostic rounds (Codex, 2026-10-16/17, one-shot BSP NMI
snapshot, not part of this patch) found a page fault in acpi.sys's early
boot IRQ arbitration (C0000005 at acpi.sys RVA 9AAA, later STOP 1E) for a
device holding a boot interrupt before the PCI IRQ routing interface
exists: first the HPET (PNP0103), then, with the HPET hidden, the AMD GPIO
controller _SB.GPIO (AMDI0030, IRQ 7 level/active-low/shared, MMIO
FED81500). Windows 2000 has a driver for neither. With both hidden the
X470 reached the 2000 desktop (user report 2026-10-17, firmware built
from Codex's diagnostic series, same table result as this patch).

Only with csmwrap.ini acpi_legacy_fadt = true (written only for the
Windows 2000 ACPI flow, tools/xp_csmwrap_esp.sh), in the private DSDT/SSDT
copies that patch 0006 already makes for that flow (RSDT view only; the
firmware tables and the XSDT view are unchanged):

- src/usos_w2k_hide.inc (new, own code from the ACPI 6.5 AML grammar,
  chapter 20; not an interpreter, nothing is executed): a Device whose
  TermList is flat (NameOp / MethodOp terms only), with exactly one _HID
  (Name, string or EISA ID integer), at most one _CID (Name: one ID or a
  Package of IDs) and exactly one _STA (Name with an integer or a method
  without arguments) qualifies if its _HID or a _CID entry is PNP0103,
  AMDI0030 or AMD0030. Its _STA becomes 0 in place: Name -> Zero / prefix
  with value 0, method body -> Return (Zero) padded with Noop. Lengths,
  names and resources stay; only _STA bytes and the checksum change.
  _HID/_CID methods, duplicates, devices inside If/Method bodies and
  anything unrecognised are left alone.
- src/usos_acpi10.c aml_legacy_copy(): dry run on the firmware table
  (never writes; only a table with a valid checksum; a malformed package
  length anywhere on the walked path counts the whole table as 0), so a
  table is copied when it has QWord windows above 4 GiB (patch 0006) or a
  device to hide. On the copy the same walk must find the same devices,
  else the copy is restored and nothing is hidden. Logged as
  "USOS W2KHIDE"; result flags USOS_ACPI10_HPET_HIDDEN 0x200 and
  USOS_ACPI10_GPIO_HIDDEN 0x400.

On the X470 DSDT (local dump, not distributed) the copy differs only in
the HPET _STA body (31 bytes), the GPIO _STA body (17 bytes) and the
checksum: byte-identical to the table Codex's X470-tested firmware gave
(SHA-256 9a918db5...). QEMU q35: nothing to hide (its DSDT has no
PNP0103/AMDI0030 device in a recognised shape), behaviour as usos10.

Not in this patch: the 0011 tick watcher (diagnostic, dropped from the
usos11 series, file kept in 3.1.2-usos10/patches), Codex's NMI snapshot
helper and its csmwrap.ini options, and the Intel GPIO rules (INT34xx /
INTC10xx; held back until tested on real Intel boards).

